# The parts that cross a document, which is the `callActivity` population and not the whole part
# table. One row per part whose target lives in another filing.
#
# The restriction is in the file name
#   Because it is not in the key, and a relation that keeps one kind of row must say so. One law
#   counting call activities and sub-processes together against the parts would let a local part be
#   drawn as a flow node. A local fusion divides the composed layer; it is not an activity inside
#   it, so the two halves are two laws.
#
# Splitting them measures `invents`
#   Only a foreign part goes through `calledElement`, which is a QName of a process, and that
#   flattening can put a loop into the emitted call graph that the parts do not have. A law over
#   both together cannot say how much of the emission is exposed to it. This one can: it is the
#   exposed population.
-- diagrams/calls.sqlc pinned to the parts that leave their own document.
SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer, c.part_notation, c.part_filing, c.part_layer
FROM (
    :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc)
) c
WHERE NOT c.is_local
